      "text": "To find the length of \\( QS \\) in the given right triangle \\( \\triangle SQR \\) with \\( \\cos R = \\frac{4}{9} \\), we will use the definition of cosine in a right triangle. The cosine of an angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the adjacent side to the hypotenuse.\n\nGiven:\n- \\( \\cos R = \\frac{4}{9} \\)\n- The adjacent side \\( QR \\) to angle \\( R \\) is 9.\n\nWe need to find the hypotenuse \\( SR \\). Using the definition of cosine:\n\\[\n\\cos R = \\frac{QR}{SR} = \\frac{9}{SR} = \\frac{4}{9}\n\\]\n\nTo find \\( SR \\), we set up the equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{9}{SR} = \\frac{4}{9}\n\\]\n\nCross-multiplying to solve for \\( SR \\):\n\\[\n9 \\cdot 9 = 4 \\cdot SR\n\\]\n\\[\n81 = 4 \\cdot SR\n\\]\n\\[\nSR = \\frac{81}{4}\n\\]\n\nNow that we have \\( SR \\), we can find \\( QS \\). \\( QS \\) is the side opposite to angle \\( R \\) and is part of the right triangle \\( \\triangle SQR \\). Using the Pythagorean theorem:\n\\[\nSR^2 = QS^2 + QR^2\n\\]\n\nWe know \\( SR = \\frac{81}{4} \\) and \\( QR = 9 \\):\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{81}{4}\\right)^2 = QS^2 + 9^2\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{6561}{16} = QS^2 + 81\n\\]\n\nTo isolate \\( QS^2 \\), subtract 81 from both sides:\n\\[\n\\frac{6561}{16} - 81 = QS^2\n\\]\n\nConvert 81 to a fraction with a denominator of 16:\n\\[\n81 = \\frac{1296}{16}\n\\]\n\nSubtract the fractions:\n\\[\n\\frac{6561}{16} - \\frac{1296}{16} = QS^2\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{5265}{16} = QS^2\n\\]\n\nTaking the square root of both sides to solve for \\( QS \\):\n\\[\nQS = \\sqrt{\\frac{5265}{16}}\n\\]\n\\[\nQS = \\frac{\\sqrt{5265}}{4}\n\\]\n\nThus, the length of \\( QS \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{\\sqrt{5265}}{4}}\n\\]",
